Benchmarks

These benchmarks measure the switching program and then validate its output
with the workflow used for switching experiments:

    graphswitching --format graph6 | labelg -gqt | sort -u | wc -l

Legacy programs still use:

    legacy program | amtog -q | labelg -gqt | sort -u | wc -l

The runner first writes generator output to a temporary file and reports the
generator's wall, user, and system time. It then reports canonical-validation
wall time separately and checks the final number of pairwise nonisomorphic
graphs. With multiple repetitions, it reports the median of each measurement.

Requirements

  - Python 3.8 or newer
  - nauty and Traces command-line tools
  - the standard sort and wc utilities

Build and run the short suite:

    make benchmark

The runner automatically looks for both common nauty naming schemes:

    amtog, labelg
    nauty-amtog, nauty-labelg

To choose a prefix explicitly:

    make benchmark NAUTY_PREFIX=nauty-
    python3 benchmarks/run.py --nauty-prefix nauty-

For paths or nonstandard names, set both command overrides:

    AMTOG=/opt/nauty/bin/amtog \
    LABELG=/opt/nauty/bin/labelg \
    make benchmark

Suites and cases

There are three cumulative suites:

  - `short` is the default Sp(6,2) comparison and is designed to finish
    within one minute on the reference machine.
  - `full` adds every reasonably quick case: extended ordinary WQH and GM,
    all `--sym` and selected `--sym=auto` regressions, eight in-range paper
    examples, and deterministic
    paired ordinary and `--sym` witnesses for every fixed method not
    already covered by an output-producing standard graph.
  - `extrafull` runs every case. It adds the legacy Sp(4,4) WQH baseline,
    which takes roughly 11 minutes by itself in the reference environment.

The `full` and `extrafull` suites include `--sym` cases, so graphswitching
must be built with nauty support as described in INSTALL.

    make benchmark BENCHMARK_SUITE=full
    make benchmark BENCHMARK_SUITE=full BENCHMARK_RUNS=3
    make benchmark BENCHMARK_SUITE=extrafull

List the cases or run only named cases:

    python3 benchmarks/run.py --list
    make benchmark BENCHMARK_CASES="sp6-wqh-p2 paley73-wqh-p3"
    python3 benchmarks/run.py --runs 5 sp6-gm sp6-gm-packed
    python3 benchmarks/run.py gq2-4-wqh-p5 bil223-wqh-p3 sp4-4-wqh-p4

The case definitions live in cases.tsv and are ordered by input graph, so
different programs and methods on the same graph appear next to each other.
The short suite currently expects two isomorphism classes from each Sp(6,2)
benchmark. The tuple-enumeration-only p=3 cases expect zero. The GQ(2,4)
case expects one output class. The Bil(2,2,3) and Sp(4,4) counts agree with
the one-step results reported in "Switching for small strongly regular
graphs":

    https://ajc.maths.uq.edu.au/pdf/84/ajc_v84_p028.pdf

The in-range paper examples intentionally exercise complete search on the
published inputs even when no changed class is emitted. The 1,296-vertex
Hamming(4,6) example is retained as fixture data but is not executable under
the exact 967-vertex limit. The deterministic `witness-*`
cases complement them: each has a planted admissible embedding and paired
ordinary/`--sym` rows, and is checked to produce switched graphs that are
nonisomorphic to its input. Their orders are selected per method to keep the
search measurable while remaining suitable for `full`; they are not
equal-size cross-method comparisons. Together with the output-producing
standard graphs, they give positive benchmark coverage in both enumeration
modes for every implemented switching method.

For useful comparisons, run benchmarks on an otherwise idle machine and
record the compiler, CFLAGS, nauty version, CPU, and operating system.
Wall-clock timings from different machines or builds should not be compared
without that context.

GCC option tuning

Run a dedicated graphswitching tuning category under several conservative GCC
optimization profiles and recommend the fastest one for the current computer
with:

    make tune-gcc

The tuner excludes all legacy programs. Its `tuning` category contains 14
output-producing cases, covers every switching-method catalogue, and includes
both ordinary and `--sym` searches. It uses three runs by default, checks every
isomorphism-class count in the separate canonical-validation stage, ranks
profiles by the sum of generator-only median wall times, and rebuilds
graphswitching with the winner. It compares `-O2`,
`-O3`, the current `-O3 -funroll-loops` default, native versions with and
without loop unrolling, and native LTO. Native profiles use `-march=native` and
therefore produce binaries that may not run on a different CPU. Run on an
otherwise idle machine; pass tuner options through Make when needed:

    make tune-gcc TUNE_GCC_ARGS="--runs 5"
    make tune-gcc TUNE_GCC_ARGS="--suite full --runs 1"
    python3 benchmarks/tune_gcc.py --help
